  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 152 PM CDT Mon Jul 27 2026\n\nA broad upper-level ridge across the Rockies into the Great Plains \nis leading to hot weather across the western half of the CONUS. This \ncombined with humidity from southerly flow advecting moisture in \nfrom the Gulf with added agricultural contributions is leading to \nthe high heat/high humidity scenario from the last couple of days. \nGood news is that the weak frontal boundary dropping south across \neastern Nebraska and western Iowa today will help to push off some \nof the added moisture from the Gulf as well as advect in milder \ntemperatures starting tomorrow. Yes, today will be our last day of \nextreme heat, at least for this stretch, with highs tomorrow back in \nthe upper 80s to low 90s and heat indices back down below 100. \n\nThis afternoon we have a few pop-up showers and storms that have \ndeveloped along the front moving through mostly east of our Iowa \ncounties. These are occasionally clipping far eastern Shelby and \nPottawattamie Counties, though. Not expecting anything severe, but \nworst case we could see 1-1.25 inch hail. The better chance for \nstorms to impact our area will be overnight into early Tuesday. \nStorms from central Nebraska should be weakening and falling apart \nas they move into eastern Nebraska leading to greater chances (20-\n30%) over our far western counties, generally decreasing as you get \nfarther east. While these storms should be fairly weak, we could see \nstrong winds if any hold together into our area. Another scenario to \nwatch is the HRRR redevelops some scattered showers and storms along \nthe remnant boundary around 16-18Z Tuesday along and east of the \nMissouri River. These again likely won't be severe but could produce \nsome gusty winds. \n\nCloud cover will linger through Tuesday morning from the overnight \nstorms as well as any late-morning showers/storms. We should see \ngradual clearing Tuesday afternoon into the evening allowing \ntemperatures to warm into the upper 80s to low 90s. Tuesday night \ninto early Wednesday we may see another chance (20%) for showers and \nstorms clipping our southeast Nebraska counties, but latest trends \nkeep this activity south and west of our area. Pattern stays fairly \nstagnant into Wednesday, though a modest bump in humidity may be \nobserved as we see weak southerly flow move back in as the High over \nthe region starts to slide east.\n\nToward the end of the work week we see a more substantial cool-down \nahead as a trough moving out of the PacNW pushes east overtopping \nthe ridge. As this approaches, we'll see a low pressure system \ndevelop and track along the Nebraska-South Dakota state line \ncreating shower and storm chances late Wednesday night into \nThursday. This Low lingers until it gets picked up by the upper-\nlevel wave Thursday afternoon, leading to a fairly rainy day on \nThursday into Thursday night. The National Blend of Models keeps \nhighs in the upper 80s to low 90s on Thursday, likely due to \nuncertainty in coverage of storms from differences in the track \ntiming/placement of the surface Low. Greater coverage of showers and \nstorms likely would lead to cooler highs than currently predicted. \nIn any case, cooler air out of central Canada will likely move in \nbehind this system for Friday going into the weekend for a better \nreprieve from the heat. \n\nWhile we see milder temperatures Friday into Saturday, we see the \nridge start to build back up to our west, looming... Temperatures \ntrend back upward Sunday into Monday next week.",
